1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,550
In 1965, a small island was pushed out of
a country it had joined less than two

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:07,360
years earlier.

3
00:00:08,167 --> 00:00:13,897
It had no oil, no farmland, no hinterland,
and not even enough fresh water for its

4
00:00:13,907 --> 00:00:14,707
own people.

5
00:00:15,233 --> 00:00:19,133
Its own prime minister broke down on
television as he announced the news.

6
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:22,873
Today, its people are among the richest in
the world.

7
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:27,913
Its port is one of the busiest anywhere,
and its airport connects the planet.

8
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:31,573
How did a tiny country with almost nothing
become this?

9
00:00:32,467 --> 00:00:34,007
This is the story of Singapore.

10
00:00:36,233 --> 00:00:41,433
Singapore's entire land area is less than
seven hundred and fifty square kilometres.

11
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:44,653
About six million people live on it.

12
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:51,423
In 1965, Singapore's output per person was
about five hundred dollars a

13
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:52,233
year.

14
00:00:52,567 --> 00:00:53,567
By 2024,

15
00:00:54,227 --> 00:00:56,375
it was more than ninety thousand dollars.

16
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,520
That is about a hundred and eighty times
as much, before adjusting for inflation.

17
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:06,667
Few countries have ever made that journey
from poverty to wealth so quickly.

18
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:11,580
And Singapore did it without the things
that usually make countries rich:

19
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:13,920
oil, minerals, land.

20
00:01:14,833 --> 00:01:16,073
So what did it have instead?

21
00:01:18,067 --> 00:01:18,967
Start with location.

22
00:01:21,067 --> 00:01:24,007
Singapore sits at the southern tip of the
Malay Peninsula,

23
00:01:24,627 --> 00:01:28,087
where the Strait of Malacca opens toward
the South China Sea.

24
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:33,903
For centuries, ships sailing between
India, China and the Middle East have
passed

25
00:01:33,913 --> 00:01:34,913
through these waters.

26
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:41,387
In January 1819, an official of the
British East India Company,

27
00:01:41,767 --> 00:01:44,127
Stamford Raffles, arrived on the island.

28
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:47,753
Britain wanted a port on this route that
its Dutch rivals,

29
00:01:48,193 --> 00:01:51,273
the main European power in the region,
could not control.

30
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:54,733
At the time, perhaps a thousand people
lived there.

31
00:01:55,533 --> 00:02:00,143
On the sixth of February, he signed a
treaty with the local rulers that allowed
the

32
00:02:00,153 --> 00:02:01,873
British to set up a trading post.

33
00:02:02,867 --> 00:02:06,667
Singapore became a free port, open to
traders from everywhere.

34
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:13,043
Treaties signed in 1824, with the Dutch
and with the local rulers,

35
00:02:13,053 --> 00:02:14,553
made the island British.

36
00:02:15,567 --> 00:02:20,557
Merchants and workers came from China,
India, the Malay world and far beyond,

37
00:02:20,567 --> 00:02:22,127
and the town grew quickly.

38
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:26,533
Rubber and tin from the Malay Peninsula
flowed out through its harbour,

39
00:02:27,113 --> 00:02:29,473
and goods from Europe and India flowed in.

40
00:02:30,333 --> 00:02:34,893
In 1826, it became part of the Straits
Settlements,

41
00:02:35,313 --> 00:02:37,213
and ten years later their capital.

42
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:42,170
For more than a century, Singapore was a
busy colonial port,

43
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:44,300
built on one thing: trade.

44
00:02:46,233 --> 00:02:48,833
That world collapsed in February 1942.

45
00:02:50,233 --> 00:02:54,843
Japanese forces crossed the narrow Johor
Strait and attacked the island from the

46
00:02:54,853 --> 00:02:55,653
north-west.

47
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:59,660
On the fifteenth of February, the British
commander surrendered.

48
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:05,763
About eighty thousand British, Indian,
Australian and local troops became
prisoners

49
00:03:05,773 --> 00:03:06,573
of war.

50
00:03:07,267 --> 00:03:12,667
Singapore was renamed Syonan-to, and
endured three and a half years of
occupation.

51
00:03:13,767 --> 00:03:18,637
In the first weeks, thousands of Chinese
civilians were rounded up and killed in a

52
00:03:18,647 --> 00:03:20,350
purge known as Sook Ching.

53
00:03:20,967 --> 00:03:25,507
Food ran short, and many families survived
on tapioca and sweet potatoes.

54
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,880
When the British returned in September
1945,

55
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:34,140
the old idea that they could always
protect the colony was gone.

56
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,790
The years that followed brought strikes,
riots and a long struggle over who would

57
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:40,620
govern the colony.

58
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:47,050
In 1959, Singapore won self-government,
and Lee Kuan Yew of the

59
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:50,260
People's Action Party became its first
prime minister.

60
00:03:51,233 --> 00:03:55,253
His government believed the island was too
small to survive on its own.

61
00:03:56,133 --> 00:04:01,963
So in September 1963, Singapore joined the
new Federation of

62
00:04:01,973 --> 00:04:02,773
Malaysia.

63
00:04:03,367 --> 00:04:04,367
It did not last.

64
00:04:05,027 --> 00:04:10,067
There were deep political disagreements,
and deadly racial riots in 1964.

65
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,700
On the ninth of August 1965,

66
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:18,920
Singapore separated from Malaysia and
became an independent country.

67
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:22,400
Lee Kuan Yew called it a moment of
anguish.

68
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:25,720
He had believed in merger all his adult
life.

69
00:04:26,633 --> 00:04:28,473
Singapore was now a nation.

70
00:04:28,933 --> 00:04:30,933
Whether it could survive was a different
question.

71
00:04:32,833 --> 00:04:34,813
Look at what the new country had to work
with.

72
00:04:35,833 --> 00:04:39,633
The main island is only about fifty
kilometres from east to west.

73
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:45,340
Much of it was swamp, forest or crowded
town, with very little room to spare.

74
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:46,967
No oil.

75
00:04:47,087 --> 00:04:47,887
No gas.

76
00:04:48,267 --> 00:04:50,127
No minerals worth mining.

77
00:04:50,707 --> 00:04:52,447
No room for large farms.

78
00:04:53,367 --> 00:04:56,147
Worst of all, not enough water for a
growing city.

79
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,560
Since 1961 and 1962,

80
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:06,410
Singapore had depended on agreements to
buy water from the Malaysian state of
Johor,

81
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:07,560
across the strait.

82
00:05:08,367 --> 00:05:11,837
Its neighbours, Malaysia and Indonesia,
were far larger,

83
00:05:11,847 --> 00:05:14,087
and relations in the region were tense.

84
00:05:15,067 --> 00:05:18,627
Its population, about two million people,
was crowded,

85
00:05:18,987 --> 00:05:20,547
mostly poor, and growing.

86
00:05:21,567 --> 00:05:24,227
Many lived in crowded shophouses and
wooden villages,

87
00:05:24,647 --> 00:05:25,527
and jobs were scarce.

88
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:30,020
Then, in January 1968,

89
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:31,820
came a second shock.

90
00:05:32,733 --> 00:05:37,613
Britain announced it would pull its
military forces out of the region by 1971.

91
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:42,880
Its bases in Singapore generated around a
fifth of the economy.

92
00:05:43,867 --> 00:05:48,557
They employed tens of thousands of local
workers and occupied about twelve percent

93
00:05:48,567 --> 00:05:49,647
of the island's land.

94
00:05:50,567 --> 00:05:54,547
Singapore had already lost its hoped-for
common market when it left Malaysia.

95
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:58,980
Now it was about to lose its protector and
one of its biggest employers.

96
00:06:00,167 --> 00:06:01,307
The government moved fast.

97
00:06:01,887 --> 00:06:02,967
In 1967

98
00:06:03,867 --> 00:06:07,307
it began national service, to build an
army of its own.

99
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:13,790
In 1968, new labour laws limited strikes
and gave employers more control,

100
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,860
to make the island more attractive to
investors.

101
00:06:18,067 --> 00:06:23,037
And in 1971, a long-term Concept Plan
mapped out land,

102
00:06:23,047 --> 00:06:26,267
roads, housing and industry for decades
ahead.

103
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:30,760
The government had to decide what kind of
economy this island would have.

104
00:06:32,533 --> 00:06:34,809
Part of the answer was already under way.

105
00:06:35,533 --> 00:06:36,913
In 1961,

106
00:06:37,613 --> 00:06:42,343
a United Nations team led by the Dutch
economist Albert Winsemius had advised

107
00:06:42,353 --> 00:06:44,913
Singapore to industrialise, fast.

108
00:06:45,967 --> 00:06:51,117
That year, the government set up the
Economic Development Board to win
investment

109
00:06:51,127 --> 00:06:52,527
and create jobs.

110
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:58,620
Its first big project was a vast
industrial estate on swampy land at
Jurong,

111
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:00,020
in the west of the island.

112
00:07:00,933 --> 00:07:05,263
Many newly independent countries at the
time were wary of foreign multinational

113
00:07:05,273 --> 00:07:06,093
companies.

114
00:07:07,233 --> 00:07:09,073
Singapore did the opposite.

115
00:07:09,693 --> 00:07:10,753
It invited them in.

116
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:14,020
Foreign companies brought money,
technology

117
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:18,360
and know-how, and Singaporean workers
learned on the job.

118
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:24,650
Factories making clothing, electronics and
parts for global brands opened across the

119
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:25,430
island,

120
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:26,320
and jobs followed.

121
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:30,080
Oil companies built refineries here too.

122
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:34,170
Shell opened the first, on the island of
Pulau Bukom,

123
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:35,440
in 1961.

124
00:07:37,067 --> 00:07:41,947
A country with no oil of its own became
one of Asia's great refining centres.

125
00:07:42,933 --> 00:07:48,003
Trade kept growing too, and Singapore's
harbour became one of the busiest ports on

126
00:07:48,013 --> 00:07:48,813
the planet.

127
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:53,830
In 2024, it handled a record forty one
million containers,

128
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:55,620
counted in twenty foot units.

129
00:07:56,567 --> 00:08:00,877
About nine in ten are transshipped, moved
from one ship to another on the way

130
00:08:00,887 --> 00:08:01,687
somewhere else.

131
00:08:02,567 --> 00:08:03,967
In 1981,

132
00:08:04,567 --> 00:08:09,167
Changi Airport opened, making Singapore a
hub of the air as well as the sea.

133
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,360
Singapore Airlines, formed in 1972,

134
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:16,420
carried the country's name around the
world.

135
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:20,750
Over the decades, the economy climbed up
the value chain,

136
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:25,160
into chemicals, precision engineering,
finance and semiconductors.

137
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:29,000
Manufacturing is still about a fifth of
the economy,

138
00:08:29,340 --> 00:08:33,260
and around one in every ten chips made in
the world is made in Singapore.

139
00:08:35,300 --> 00:08:37,580
Investors did not come only for the
location.

140
00:08:38,633 --> 00:08:41,653
They came because the government worked,
and because it was clean.

141
00:08:42,567 --> 00:08:47,477
Civil servants were judged on results, and
the rules on bribery applied all the way

142
00:08:47,487 --> 00:08:48,287
up.

143
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:51,680
The streets were cleaned up, and littering
was punished with fines.

144
00:08:52,733 --> 00:08:58,603
An anti-corruption agency, set up in 1952,
was given tough new laws

145
00:08:58,613 --> 00:09:02,273
in 1960 to go after bribery at every
level.

146
00:09:03,167 --> 00:09:09,037
In 2024, Transparency International ranked
Singapore the third least corrupt

147
00:09:09,047 --> 00:09:10,367
country in the world.

148
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:12,040
Then there was housing.

149
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:19,450
In 1960, the government created the
Housing and Development Board to replace

150
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:21,900
overcrowded slums with modern flats.

151
00:09:22,933 --> 00:09:24,413
In May 1961,

152
00:09:25,313 --> 00:09:30,113
a huge fire in Bukit Ho Swee left about
sixteen thousand people homeless.

153
00:09:31,133 --> 00:09:34,483
The board rehoused them and kept building
at remarkable speed:

154
00:09:34,493 --> 00:09:37,733
twenty one thousand flats in less than
three years.

155
00:09:38,633 --> 00:09:39,813
From 1968,

156
00:09:40,533 --> 00:09:43,573
workers could use savings from the Central
Provident Fund,

157
00:09:44,033 --> 00:09:47,213
a compulsory savings scheme, to buy their
homes.

158
00:09:48,167 --> 00:09:52,277
Today, about seventy seven percent of
residents live in public housing,

159
00:09:52,287 --> 00:09:55,187
and around nine in ten own their home.

160
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,560
Owning a home gave ordinary people a
direct stake in the country's success.

161
00:10:00,633 --> 00:10:02,683
The state saved and invested too.

162
00:10:02,693 --> 00:10:08,003
Temasek Holdings was set up in 1974, and
GIC in

163
00:10:08,013 --> 00:10:08,703
1981,

164
00:10:08,713 --> 00:10:11,433
to manage its assets and reserves.

165
00:10:12,533 --> 00:10:17,623
And it chose English as a common working
language, alongside each community's
mother

166
00:10:17,633 --> 00:10:18,433
tongue.

167
00:10:18,833 --> 00:10:19,873
None of this was gentle.

168
00:10:20,393 --> 00:10:24,783
The government was strict, and critics
point to tight limits on the press and the

169
00:10:24,793 --> 00:10:25,593
political opposition.

170
00:10:27,733 --> 00:10:31,213
Singapore's real resource has always been
its people.

171
00:10:32,033 --> 00:10:33,213
Most are of Chinese,

172
00:10:33,853 --> 00:10:37,493
Malay or Indian descent, alongside many
other communities.

173
00:10:38,733 --> 00:10:43,643
Within a single generation, many families
moved from villages and crowded shophouses

174
00:10:43,653 --> 00:10:45,113
into high-rise flats.

175
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:48,540
Each has left its mark on the city's
neighbourhoods,

176
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:50,100
festivals and food.

177
00:10:50,933 --> 00:10:55,923
Public housing estates use ethnic quotas,
so that communities live side by side

178
00:10:55,933 --> 00:10:56,733
rather than apart.

179
00:10:57,567 --> 00:11:01,757
Hawker centres, where cheap meals from
every tradition are sold under one roof,

180
00:11:01,767 --> 00:11:04,007
became part of the national identity.

181
00:11:05,033 --> 00:11:10,323
In 2020, UNESCO recognised Singapore's
hawker culture as part of

182
00:11:10,333 --> 00:11:12,493
humanity's intangible heritage.

183
00:11:13,533 --> 00:11:19,203
Education became a national priority, and
Singapore's students now regularly score

184
00:11:19,213 --> 00:11:21,513
near the top of international tests.

185
00:11:22,633 --> 00:11:26,413
Today, about six million people live on
the island,

186
00:11:26,893 --> 00:11:30,833
and nearly a third of them are not
citizens or permanent residents.

187
00:11:32,933 --> 00:11:36,073
For a country this small, Singapore's
reach is enormous.

188
00:11:37,167 --> 00:11:40,747
Its total trade is worth more than three
times its whole economy.

189
00:11:41,633 --> 00:11:44,213
It is one of the world's leading financial
centres,

190
00:11:44,433 --> 00:11:45,973
ranked in the global top five.

191
00:11:46,933 --> 00:11:50,813
Hundreds of global companies run their
Asian operations from the island.

192
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:55,140
And Changi has been voted the world's best
airport many times.

193
00:11:56,133 --> 00:11:56,953
Its shipping lanes,

194
00:11:57,613 --> 00:12:01,313
airport and banks tie Southeast Asia to
the rest of the world.

195
00:12:02,133 --> 00:12:06,193
And it works hard to stay on good terms
with both the United States and China.

196
00:12:08,133 --> 00:12:10,333
Success has brought problems of its own.

197
00:12:11,133 --> 00:12:16,083
Singapore is regularly ranked among the
most expensive cities in the world to live

198
00:12:16,093 --> 00:12:16,893
in.

199
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,940
Its people are having very few children.

200
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:25,060
In 2023, the fertility rate fell below one
child per woman for the first time.

201
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:29,570
About one in five citizens is now sixty
five or older,

202
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:32,760
and by 2030 it will be one in four.

203
00:12:33,567 --> 00:12:38,007
Land is still scarce, and the economy
relies heavily on foreign workers.

204
00:12:39,033 --> 00:12:43,283
Housing prices have climbed, and many
young couples worry about whether they can

205
00:12:43,293 --> 00:12:44,093
afford a home.

206
00:12:44,867 --> 00:12:49,147
And as a low-lying island, Singapore is
exposed to rising seas,

207
00:12:49,607 --> 00:12:52,787
which could rise by up to a metre by the
end of the century.

208
00:12:53,700 --> 00:12:59,590
In 2019, the prime minister said
protecting the coast could cost a hundred
billion

209
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:02,760
Singapore dollars or more over the coming
decades.

210
00:13:03,767 --> 00:13:08,617
And some Singaporeans ask whether a system
built for growth leaves enough room for

211
00:13:08,627 --> 00:13:09,427
dissent.

212
00:13:10,833 --> 00:13:15,793
So Singapore is doing what it has always
done: planning decades ahead.

213
00:13:16,667 --> 00:13:21,137
It has grown its own land by more than a
quarter since the 1960s,

214
00:13:21,147 --> 00:13:22,747
by reclaiming it from the sea.

215
00:13:23,533 --> 00:13:27,583
Off the island of Pulau Tekong, it has
built its first polder,

216
00:13:27,593 --> 00:13:31,833
Dutch style: land below sea level,
protected by a wall.

217
00:13:32,733 --> 00:13:34,953
The new land there covers about eight
hundred hectares.

218
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:41,610
A giant new port is rising at Tuas,
designed to handle sixty five million
containers

219
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:44,900
a year when it is finished in the 2040s.

220
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:49,800
The port is being built on more than
thirteen hundred hectares of land,

221
00:13:50,180 --> 00:13:51,680
much of it reclaimed from the sea.

222
00:13:52,533 --> 00:13:57,703
At Changi, a fifth terminal is under
construction, planned to open in the mid

223
00:13:57,713 --> 00:13:59,093
2030s.

224
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:05,160
Terminal 5 alone is designed to serve
around fifty million passengers a year.

225
00:14:05,933 --> 00:14:11,183
For water, it has built its own supply:
rainwater, recycled water called

226
00:14:11,193 --> 00:14:12,023
NEWater,

227
00:14:12,033 --> 00:14:13,513
and desalination plants.

228
00:14:14,567 --> 00:14:18,187
The first water agreement with Malaysia
ended in 2011.

229
00:14:18,907 --> 00:14:22,274
The last one runs until 2061.

230
00:14:23,867 --> 00:14:28,127
Singapore began with no oil, no farmland
and not enough water.

231
00:14:28,900 --> 00:14:33,050
What it had was a location, and leaders
who treated that location as a starting

232
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:33,860
point,

233
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:34,720
not a guarantee.

234
00:14:35,700 --> 00:14:39,100
It invited the world in, housed its
people, fought corruption,

235
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:40,800
and saved for the future.

236
00:14:41,633 --> 00:14:45,283
Its success came from choices: to stay
open, to plan ahead,

237
00:14:45,293 --> 00:14:49,093
and to keep its promises to investors and
to its own people.

238
00:14:49,867 --> 00:14:50,847
None of it was inevitable.

239
00:14:51,527 --> 00:14:52,747
In 1965,

240
00:14:53,607 --> 00:14:55,747
even its founders were not sure it would
survive.

241
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:57,660
Resources matter.

242
00:14:58,080 --> 00:15:02,720
But Singapore shows that what a country
does with its position can matter even
more.
